> Simply replacing the HDR10 branch with `color.rgb = linear_to_srgb(color.rgb);` leads to the low precision image looking even darker than it already did and the high precision image still looking washed out but thanks for the suggestion anyway.

Just to ensure there wasn't a silly miscommunication, did you try replacing `color.rgb *= data.reference_multiplier;` with `color.rgb = linear_to_srgb(color.rgb);` in the `SRGB_LINEAR` branch so that it looks a little like this?

```
if (data.target_color_space == COLOR_SPACE_SRGB_LINEAR) {
	// Negative values may be interpreted as scRGB colors,
	// so clip them to the intended sRGB colors.
	color.rgb = max(vec3(0.0), color.rgb);
	if (data.source_is_srgb == true) {
		// sRGB -> linear conversion
		color.rgb = srgb_to_linear(color.rgb);
	}

	color.rgb = linear_to_srgb(color.rgb);
```

Since you're having issues with the "high precision", this means the `if (data.target_color_space == COLOR_SPACE_SRGB_LINEAR)` branch is the one in question and might be actually an SDR sRGB buffer.

(In my case, it was the `COLOR_SPACE_HDR10_ST2084` branch that was having issue, but you mentioned this one was not an issue for you...)

> You're not supposed to use the luminance values of the transfer function, but rather min_lum and max_lum from the target_luminance event.

I don't know how the tonemappers are meant to be using max luminance to do tonemapping I am unfortunately not able to tell what the difference between the transfer function luminances and the target luminances mean. The best results I have gotten so far is using only transfer function luminances but if using target luminances could improve something I guess I need to try. 

From what I've been able to find luminances are only used (internally) in 2 places: `tonemap.glsl` and `blit.glsl`

Inside `tonemap.glsl` we use `output_max_value` which is `max_lum / sdr_white` and used to adjust the reinhard tonemapping and something about glow.

Inside `blit.glsl` we use `reference_multiplier` to multiply the brightness of the final output by `sdr_white / max_lum` (by default it was `sdr_white / 100` but my change made high precision look better). With scRGB output, the multiplier is applied after the gamma22 -> linear conversion. With HDR10 output it is applied between gamma22 -> linear and linear -> st2084.

Since reinhard is more complex I'm putting it after mentioning the 2 places

```
vec3 tonemap_reinhard(vec3 color, float white) {
	float white_squared = white * white;

	// Updated version of the Reinhard tonemapper supporting HDR rendering.
	return color * (1.0f + color / (white_squared / params.output_max_value)) / (1.0f + color / params.output_max_value);
}
```

Reinhard tonemapper (SDR only)
```
vec3 tonemap_reinhard(vec3 color, float white) {
	float white_squared = white * white;
	vec3 white_squared_color = white_squared * color;
	// Equivalent to color * (1 + color / white_squared) / (1 + color)
	return (white_squared_color + color * color) / (white_squared_color + white_squared);
}
```

I suppose I should've read how reinhard works by now and how this `max_value` concept applies to it. Maybe then I would understand what the difference between the transfer function luminance and the target luminance means. I will make sure to do that and also follow up on the matrix as to what `output_max_value` and `reference_multiplier` are meant to represent to see if I can get a better grasp on things.

And, simply throwing stuff out there, would both the tonemapper and the blit shader (where the tf is applied) using the same max luminance cause problems?

